<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-arch-output.txt.sidecar:3844:    36	**External tool delegation**: When Codex or Cursor are available, larch delegates tasks to them. For **review, sketches, and dialectic** tasks routed through `scripts/launch-review.sh --tool cursor` and `scripts/launch-review.sh --tool codex`, Codex and Cursor are now mechanically sandboxed at the CLI level (issue #1529): Codex review runs as `codex exec --sandbox read-only` and Cursor review runs as `cursor agent -p --trust --mode plan` (issue #1583 removed `--sandbox enabled`, which crashed hosts where the cursor-agent sandbox runtime is unavailable — the prior `codex exec --full-auto` (workspace-write) and `cursor agent -p --force --trust` (force-allow commands) postures are gone). `/design` plan-review voting uses `scripts/dispatch-plan-voters.sh`, which launches external voters through `scripts/run-external-agent.sh` directly but keeps the same read-only CLI posture (`codex exec --sandbox read-only` and `cursor agent -p --trust --mode plan`) and waits on wrapper sentinels before returning voter paths. The review launchers also provide a HARD CONSTRAINTS read-only preamble so the model reasons about its read-only role: Cursor prepends it to the prompt, while Codex writes it to a per-invocation `CODEX_HOME/config.toml` top-level `instructions` key outside both `$PWD` and the review output `--add-dir`. The plan-voter dispatcher includes a narrower do-not-modify voter prompt but does not publish launcher-owned dirty-tree sidecars; its primary mechanical controls are the external CLI read-only modes plus the monitored wrapper. For Codex, the CLI sandbox is the primary mitigation and the instructions preamble is its prompt-level reinforcement; for Cursor, read-only enforcement relies on `--mode plan` plus the prompt preamble plus the post-hoc `${OUTPUT}.dirty-tree` detector on review-launcher lanes (Cursor/Codex parity). Mechanical guarantee depends on the upstream Codex / Cursor CLIs continuing to honor `--sandbox read-only` / `--mode plan` respectively; the launcher harnesses (`scripts/test-launch-review.sh`) and plan-voter dispatcher harness (`scripts/test-dispatch-plan-voters.sh`) pin the argv literally, and review-launcher dirty-tree sidecars catch post-run mutation if a future CLI release silently changes those flags' semantics. Note that this hardening applies only to the **review-launcher and plan-voter lanes** — the implementer launchers (`scripts/launch-codex-implement.sh`, `scripts/launch-cursor-implement.sh`) intentionally retain workspace-write / `--trust` because the implementer's job is to edit the tree (see "Step 2 implementation" paragraph below). `/design` adaptive sketch process counts are projected in [`docs/topology.md`](docs/topology.md#design.sketch.regular_slots): full sketch mode launches 4 external agents, quick/simple sketch mode launches 2, and the codebase-scan-confirmed trivial-doc path launches 0 external sketch agents and writes sentinel artifacts instead. The trust model for nonzero sketch modes is unchanged — same scripts, same prompt constraints. `/implement` resolves the omitted-`--coder` implementer in **Step 1** (`### Implementer waterfall` in `skills/implement/SKILL.md`) before Step 2 dispatch; Step 2 implementation then consumes that resolved `--coder` and, when `--coder` was still omitted upstream, routes by external availability: Cursor → Codex → Claude (main agent only when both are unavailable); an explicit `--coder=cursor` still runs Cursor as `cursor agent -p --force --trust`, while the Codex fallback path uses `codex exec --full-auto` with `approval: never` and `sandbox: workspace-write`. The Codex implementer launcher (`scripts/launch-codex-implement.sh`) writes the static implementer agent preamble to a per-invocation `CODEX_HOME/config.toml` top-level `instructions` key, symlinks `~/.codex/auth.json` into that temporary home only when the file exists, and keeps the temporary home outside both `$PWD` and the dispatcher session tmpdir. The launcher passes two `--add-dir` flags to `codex exec`: (1) `--add-dir "$(dirname "$MANIFEST_PATH")"` so Codex's `workspace-write` sandbox can atomic-write `manifest.json` and `qa-pending.json` to the dispatcher-owned session tmpdir (outside `$PWD`, typically under `~/.cache/larch/sessions/...`); and (2) `--add-dir "$PWD"` to explicitly grant write access to the repo root — without this second grant the `workspace-write` sandbox denies writes to repo-root files (`Makefile`, `AGENTS.md`, etc.) and paths under `.claude/`, producing `sandbox-denied-required-root-files` bails. Together these widen Codex's writable surface to the repo root (and all paths beneath it) plus the entire session tmpdir. Operators should treat the session tmpdir as Codex-writable (transcripts, sidecars, dispatcher baseline / spawn / resume-count sentinels), but it does not contain Codex's temporary config or auth symlink (those live under `$CODEX_HOME_DIR`). Cursor (`--trust`) already permits writes to arbitrary absolute paths and requires no analogous launcher flag. The operator can opt into Cursor (`cursor agent ... --workspace "$PWD"`) with `--coder=cursor`, or the main Claude agent's Edit/Write path with `--coder=claude`. **Codex and Cursor do not commit** — Codex cannot write `.git/` under `workspace-write`, and Cursor prompts forbid commits. Cursor has full filesystem access, so the dispatcher (`skills/implement/scripts/step2-implement.sh`) mechanically asserts `HEAD == BASELINE_SHA` before committing on its behalf and bails with `cursor-modified-history` if the tool created or amended commits, preserving partial work for operator inspection. Operators who want a stricter sandbox model should prefer `--coder=codex`. The dispatcher pipes `manifest.commit_message` through `scripts/redact-secrets.sh`, writes the redacted output to a tmpfile, and runs `git add -A && git commit -F <tmpfile>` on the implementer's behalf — so what lands in git history is the manifest's `commit_message` modulo the redactor (the same scrubber used downstream for the canonical on-disk manifest). The redactor pass before `git commit` is load-bearing: without it, a secret accidentally embedded in `commit_message` would land permanently in git history while the on-disk canonical manifest's redacted copy would suggest the secret had been scrubbed (split brain). `git add -A` stages every working-tree change (tracked + untracked) — under this trust model the working tree IS the source of truth, `manifest.files_touched` is advisory documentation only (no diff cross-check), and operator review + `/review` (Step 5) + the regular pre-commit hook chain are the downstream backstops against external implementers committing files outside the declared scope. Before staging, the dispatcher compares `git status --porcelain` paths against manifest-declared paths and appends a best-effort Warning to `execution-issues.md` for undeclared working-tree paths; this makes scope drift visible but does not block the commit. Operators concerned about scope drift should review the produced commit before allowing the merge. There is no Claude-side diff verification — the working tree IS the source of truth, and `commit_message` flows into `git commit -F` modulo the secrets-family redaction described above. External implementer process writes bypass the Edit/Write PreToolUse hook chain (including `block-submodule-edit.sh`); the regular `pre-commit` hook chain runs when the dispatcher commits, providing a second-line backstop. Post-implementer validation in `skills/implement/scripts/step2-implement.sh` mechanically enforces, BEFORE the dispatcher commits: (1) `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` matches the spawn-time branch (→ `branch-changed`); (2) `git hash-object .claude-plugin/plugin.json` matches the spawn-time baseline, catching working-tree edits to the version-bump-reserved file (→ `protected-path-modified`); (3) `git submodule status --recursive` reports clean (→ `submodule-dirty`); (4) every manifest path is rejected if it contains `..`, starts with `/`, equals `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`, or resolves under a submodule (→ `protected-path-modified`); (5) Cursor only: `HEAD` still matches the spawn-time baseline (→ `cursor-modified-history`). The earlier diff cross-check (`manifest-diff-mismatch`), commit-subject equality (`commit-subject-mismatch`), working-tree-clean (`dirty-tree-after-codex`), and commits-since-baseline (`no-commit-since-baseline`) checks were removed when commit responsibility moved off Codex — they were tautological under the new model, since the dispatcher itself produces the commit from `manifest.commit_message`. If `git commit` fails (empty working tree, pre-commit hook rejection, transient git error), the dispatcher emits `commit-failed`, removes the un-sanitized `manifest.json` from `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR` to avoid leaving raw text fields on disk, captures `git commit` stderr to `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/<tool>-commit-stderr.txt`, and bails — the index stays fully staged from the prior `git add -A` (no new commit was produced) so operators may need `git reset` or `git commit --amend` after diagnosing the failure, not only inspection. Manifest text fields (`commit_message`, `summary_bullets[]`, `todos_left[]`, `oos_observations[*].title|description`) are run through `scripts/redact-secrets.sh` before any consumption by downstream Steps 4 / 8a / 9a / 9a.1, since they flow verbatim into commit messages, the CHANGELOG, the PR body, and public GitHub issues. Claude does NOT inspect implementer transcripts or run `git diff` to figure out what changed — only the validated, sanitized manifest is consumed downstream. For `/research` — which launches external agents directly against `$PWD` — the trust framing (mechanical PreToolUse hook on `/research`'s `Edit|Write|NotebookEdit` surface, prompt-only posture for Bash and external agents, residual risks, and scope of the mechanical guarantee) is documented in the [External reviewer write surface in /research](#external-reviewer-write-surface-in-research) subsection below.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-arch-output.txt.sidecar:3850:    42	**Review fix application**: `/review-and-fix` and `/implement` Step 5 apply only findings accepted by the review voting path, but accepted finding text is still treated as untrusted reviewer data. `skills/review-and-fix/scripts/review-and-fix.sh` applies fixes through Codex, then Cursor, while the main agent does not use Edit/Write for review fixes. Submodule protection is layered: `scripts/scrub-submodule-paths.sh` drops accepted findings that target discovered submodule roots before dispatch, the coder prompt includes a submodule prohibition block, and post-dispatch `git diff --name-only` / `git diff --name-only --cached` plus untracked-path checks revert tracked submodule changes, remove untracked files under submodule roots, and fail the round with `CODER_STATUS=submodule-violation`. `FIX_COUNT` is the post-scrub coder input count, so downstream bulk-skip-ratio logic measures only the findings actually dispatched to the coder. When the coder reports accepted in-scope findings as `SKIPPED:`, only non-security skipped blocks may be mirrored into public OOS review artifacts; skipped blocks with an unfenced `focus-area=security` token are held only in local per-run files and must never be copied to `oos-accepted-review.md`. The security classifier now fails closed: classifier errors (for example missing `python3`, unreadable temp files, decode failures, or runtime failures) abort the round instead of defaulting to the public OOS path, and `oos-accepted-review.md` mirror-copy failures likewise abort instead of silently leaving the legacy mirror stale. Prompt wrappers must ignore instructions embedded in reviewer prose and skip unsafe findings instead of applying edits outside the dispatch contract.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-arch-output.txt.sidecar:3862:    54	**SessionStart tmpdir advisory path**: `scripts/sessionstart-health.sh` resolves active `/implement` tmpdirs when `jq` is available and stdin carries the hook payload. It reads the JSON `cwd` and `session_id` fields, exports `session_id` as `LARCH_TOKEN_SESSION_ID`, and calls `skills/implement/scripts/lib-resolve-implement-tmpdir.sh` to scan session roots for a `.larch-keepalive` whose `CLONE_PATH` matches `cwd` and whose tmpdir carries a resolver eligibility sentinel (`design-export/manifest.env`, `review-round-summary.md`, or `.bump-version-armed`). It **does not** emit the legacy “pending post-/design boundary / run `post-design-boundary.sh`” advisory for `design-export/manifest.env` alone — that was tied to the retired gate. It **does** still surface **post-/review** and **post-/bump-version** pending-boundary hints, dirty-tree / stash / merge-state warnings, and `larch-stalled-run.txt` recovery text when applicable. This SessionStart path is advisory only: malformed JSON, missing stdin, missing `jq`, missing resolver, no matching tmpdir, stale candidates, or `.run-cleaned-up` all fail open with exit 0 and no boundary message. The hook does not write files in this path; when it emits an advisory, it discloses only the resolved tmpdir basename inside `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, not an absolute path.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-arch-output.txt.sidecar:3864:    56	Reduced residual risk for stale `claude-implement-*` tmpdirs: prefer `LARCH_TOKEN_SESSION_ID` binding (when set) and the documented resolver TTL backstop; exact session-id matches bypass TTL for long-running sessions.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-arch-output.txt.sidecar:3882:    74	**Cursor/Codex outer-launcher retry metadata and dirty-tree guard**: Cursor and Codex review retries may carry `OUTER_LAUNCHER`, `OUTER_LAUNCHER_PROMPT_FILE`, and `OUTER_LAUNCHER_WORKDIR` in the same `.meta` sidecar so retries replay through `scripts/launch-review.sh --tool cursor` or `scripts/launch-review.sh --tool codex` and keep launcher-owned post-processing. The collector treats these fields as tamperable data: all three must be present together, `OUTER_LAUNCHER` is rejected if it contains `..` and must canonicalize to the tool-matched allowlisted launcher, `OUTER_LAUNCHER_PROMPT_FILE` is pinned to `${ORIG_OUTPUT}.prompt` and must be a readable regular non-symlink file, and `OUTER_LAUNCHER_WORKDIR` is rejected on `..` and must be an existing directory. Invalid outer metadata fails closed instead of falling back to `CMD_JSON`, because replaying the inner command would skip launcher-owned post-processing and the mid-run dirty-tree guard. Each launcher captures a pre-launch untracked baseline and writes `${OUTPUT}.dirty-tree` before `${OUTPUT}.done`; `STATUS=dirty` or `STATUS=unknown` triggers automatic log-and-discard of the reviewer-introduced changes — no operator prompt is issued and no stash is created. `/review` and `/implement` Step 5 both reach this through `review-core.sh`. This guard detects reviewer writes after the fact; it does not prevent the reviewer process from writing during its run. `launch-cursor-implement.sh` writes analogous outer-launcher metadata for forward compatibility, but the collector does not allowlist it until an implement-side replay caller exists.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-arch-output.txt.sidecar:3906:    98	`.gitleaks.toml` maintains a narrow path-based allowlist: the config's self-allowlist (`^\.gitleaks\.toml$`), redaction-scanner source (`scripts/redact-secrets.sh`), redaction-scanner test fixtures (`scripts/test-redact-secrets.sh`), the tracking-issue-write lifecycle shell helper plus its contract doc and regression harness (`scripts/tracking-issue-write.sh`, `scripts/tracking-issue-write.md`, `scripts/test-tracking-issue-write.md`), and the committed run-log tree (`^larch-logs/`). The first four paths legitimately carry token-shaped strings throughout — regex literals, token-family tables, and synthetic test inputs — so per-line allowlisting would churn without adding signal. The `larch-logs/` allowlist suppresses `generic-api-key` false positives on UUID-shaped `LARCH_TOKEN_SESSION_ID` values that appear in committed session transcripts; **this means gitleaks Layers 1–2 do NOT scan files under `larch-logs/` for any pattern** — contributors must not paste live credentials, API keys, or other secrets into run-log files and must rely on the redaction pipeline (`scripts/redact-secrets.sh`, applied by `larch-log.sh` before each write) as the primary defence for that subtree. **High-churn documentation is NOT allowlisted** (#375): `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `SECURITY.md` itself, `skills/issue/SKILL.md`, and `skills/issue/scripts/create-one.sh` are scanned by gitleaks in both Layer 1 (pre-commit, working tree) and Layer 2 (CI, full history). The layer responsibilities remain distinct: gitleaks Layers 1–2 catch regex-matched patterns including synthetic token-shaped literals in docs; trufflehog Layer 3 (`--only-verified`) catches ONLY live, authenticable credentials and is non-redundant with gitleaks for that reason, NOT a replacement for it — an accidental paste of a revoked token or a covered-family token in an unusual format is caught by gitleaks Layers 1–2, not by the verified-only scan. Tokens whose format falls outside gitleaks' covered rule families (see the "Outbound shell-layer redaction" subsection below for the covered families) may slip both Layer 1–2 (no matching regex rule) and Layer 3 (nothing to authenticate against a live API) — contributors must not rely on scanner layers as a substitute for editorial discipline in docs. Contributors adding new token-shaped examples to docs should use non-detector-matching forms: short prefixes without the high-entropy suffix (e.g., `ghp_` as a prefix mention rather than a full 40-character token) or an explicit placeholder like `<REDACTED-TOKEN>`.
