<TMPDIR>/codex-sketch-innovation-output.txt.meta:6:CMD_JSON=["codex","exec","--sandbox","read-only","-C","<OPERATOR_REPO_PATH>","--add-dir","<TMPDIR>","-m","gpt-5.5","-c","model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"","-c","projects.\"/Users/zhupanov/larch5\".trust_level=\"trusted\"","--output-last-message","<TMPDIR>/codex-sketch-innovation-output.txt","--","You are an Innovation/Exploration architect. Propose a high-level implementation approach for this feature: feat(/design): append [PLANNED] to issue title on completion + branch/logs/PR flow\n\n1. When design process completes (plan written to GitHub issue), append [PLANNED] prefix to the tracking issue title, consistent with [IN PROGRESS], [DONE], [STALLED] bracket convention in tracking-issue-write.sh.\n2. /design should create a branch when it starts the process, save all run logs (all DESIGN_TMPDIR content) directly in its branch's larch-logs/design/<run-id>/ directory just like where /implement flushes its logs, and, after completing its work, create a PR that bypasses CI (using [skip ci] in commit message and gh pr merge --squash --admin) and merge it which contains complete run logs of /design. Delete the branch after merge.\n\nRelevant codebase context:\n- tracking-issue-write.sh rename subcommand supports states: in-progress|done|stalled. Add 'planned' state.\n- lib-title-markers.sh has strip_lifecycle_prefix() function; add [PLANNED] case.\n- scripts/larch-log.sh commit verb does git add + commit to larch-logs/<skill>/<run-id>/ but hardcodes message without [skip ci].\n- /design Step 1 already creates a branch with create-branch.sh.\n- /design Step 5 writes the plan to GitHub issue via plan-block-write.sh, then cleans up DESIGN_TMPDIR.\n- The log flush + PR creation should happen in Step 5 AFTER plan-block-write.sh and BEFORE cleanup-tmpdir.sh.\n- Branch should be deleted after merge.\n- This feature is standalone /design only (no nested mode guard needed).. Your role is to question assumptions, suggest creative alternatives, and propose unconventional solutions that others might not consider. Explore the codebase at /Users/zhupanov/larch5. Write 2-3 paragraphs covering: (1) Key architectural decisions — emphasize any novel approaches or alternatives to the obvious path, (2) Which files/modules to modify and why, (3) Main tradeoffs including any 'crazy but might work' ideas worth considering. Do NOT modify files.\n"]
