<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3133:./larch-logs/implement/F17AB4A1-6889-4B78-8965-EE6C703494CC/round-1/review-round-summary.md:29:### FINDING_5: Automated tests omit the planned probe-failure / exit-2 regression case
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3134:./larch-logs/implement/F17AB4A1-6889-4B78-8965-EE6C703494CC/round-1/aggregator-output.txt:24:### FINDING_5: Automated tests omit the planned probe-failure / exit-2 regression case
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3135:./larch-logs/implement/F17AB4A1-6889-4B78-8965-EE6C703494CC/code-review-tally.json:1:{"schema_version":1,"phase":"code-review","batch":"code-review-tally","mode":"hard","rounds":1,"accepted_count":6,"rejected_count":1,"exonerated_count":2,"neutral_count":0,"body":"Rounds: 1 | Accepted: 6 | Rejected: 1 | Exonerated: 2 | Neutral: 0\n\n# Review Round 1\n\n- Mode: `diff`\n\n## Accepted Findings\n\n### FINDING_1: Git log/diff probes can fail silently and authorize a destructive reset\n- **Reviewer(s)**: cursor-specialist-structure-output.txt, cursor-specialist-correctness-output.txt, cursor-specialist-security-output.txt, cursor-specialist-edge-cases-output.txt, cursor-specialist-plan-fidelity-output.txt\n- **Concern**: `git log` / `git diff` pipelines suppress errors (for example via `|| true` / empty reads), leaving “all flush” / “larch-logs-only” flags true by default; with `AHEAD>0`, transient or partial git failures can be misclassified as safe flush-only work and trigger `git reset --hard origin/main`, discarding real local commits that were never validated.\n- **Suggested revision**: Stop masking probe failures on the destructive path; capture and check subprocess exit status; when `AHEAD>0` and probes fail or are ambiguous, emit `SYNC_STATUS=probe-error` and exit `2` before any reset (optionally tighten with parity checks such as subject/path line counts versus `AHEAD`).\n\n\n### FINDING_2: Reset success is not verified; callers can continue in an inconsistent state\n- **Reviewer(s)**: cursor-specialist-structure-output.txt, cursor-specialist-edge-cases-output.txt\n- **Concern**: Integration between `scripts/preflight.sh` and `scripts/check-main-sync.sh` can treat a reset as successful when it was not: `git reset --hard` may be silenced / unchecked while `SYNC_STATUS` still reflects success, so a failed reset (hooks, I/O, lock) or other unexpected git exit combined with permissive error handling can leave the repo on an ambiguous local state while the workflow proceeds (for example toward rebase).\n- **Suggested revision**: Check `git reset --hard` exit status, surface stderr on failure, and map failure to `probe-error` / a non-success `SYNC_STATUS` (or an explicit blocked path) so preflight and downstream steps do not continue as if `origin/main` was restored.\n\n\n### FINDING_3: Main-sync can be skipped when the helper is not executable, weakening the lock path\n- **Reviewer(s)**: cursor-specialist-structure-output.txt, cursor-specialist-correctness-output.txt, cursor-specialist-edge-cases-output.txt\n- **Concern**: `skills/fix-issue/scripts/find-lock-issue.sh` gates running `check-main-sync` on the script being executable; broken packaging, sparse checkouts, or stripped execute bits can skip main-sync entirely while locking still proceeds, silently removing ahead-of-origin protection.\n- **Suggested revision**: Fail closed (for example `ELIGIBLE=false` with an explicit error), invoke via `bash` on the script path, and/or enforce executable-bit guarantees in packaging/CI so the lock path cannot bypass sync checks.\n\n\n### FINDING_5: Automated tests omit the planned probe-failure / exit-2 regression case\n- **Reviewer(s)**: cursor-specialist-structure-output.txt, cursor-specialist-correctness-output.txt, cursor-specialist-testing-output.txt, cursor-specialist-edge-cases-output.txt, cursor-specialist-plan-fidelity-output.txt\n- **Concern**: The implementation plan calls for a probe-failure scenario (`SYNC_STATUS=probe-error`, exit `2`), but `scripts/test-check-main-sync.sh` / related docs do not exercise it, so regressions in probe handling, `rev-list` edge cases, and caller mapping for exit `2` can ship without CI signal.\n- **Suggested revision**: Add a controlled fixture (for example git wrapper, invalid `GIT_DIR`, or sterile repo) asserting stdout/`SYNC_STATUS` and exit code `2`, and align `scripts/test-check-main-sync.md` with the harness.\n\n\n### FINDING_7: Documentation overclaims generic git probe failures vs what the code actually fail-closes\n- **Reviewer(s)**: cursor-specialist-correctness-output.txt\n- **Concern**: `scripts/check-main-sync.md` documents a generic git probe failure contract (exit `2`) that may not match narrower fail-closed behavior in code (called out as `rev-list`-only in the review), misleading maintainers about which probes are safety-critical.\n- **Suggested revision**: Align the documented contract with the implemented checks, or extend the code to satisfy the documented contract.\n\n\n### FINDING_8: Auto-reset combined with `--skip-clean-check` can destroy dirty tracked work before rebase\n- **Reviewer(s)**: cursor-specialist-edge-cases-output.txt\n- **Concern**: On a dirty tracked tree, using `--skip-clean-check` with flush-only ahead commits can still allow `check-main-sync`’s `git reset --hard`, wiping local tracked edits that preflight previously preserved when rebase would fail, changing lossiness of the workflow.\n- **Suggested revision**: Gate destructive reset on a clean working tree (or on `SKIP_CLEAN_CHECK=false`), or refuse reset when `git status --porcelain` is non-empty.\n\n\n\n\n## Rejected Code Review Findings\n\n\nRound 1\n\n\n### [rejected] FINDING_4\n\n### FINDING_4: Documentation misstates fetch timing for the find-lock vs preflight callers\n- **Reviewer(s)**: cursor-specialist-structure-output.txt\n- **Concern**: `scripts/check-main-sync.md` wording implies the wrong relative timing of fetch versus no-fetch callers, which can mislead operators about whether `origin/main` is fresh or stale on the find-lock path versus preflight.\n- **Suggested revision**: Rewrite the sentence(s) so the documented fetch behavior matches each caller’s actual behavior.\n\n\nVote tally: YES=1 NO=2 EXON=0 JUDGE_ERROR=0\n\n\n\n\n\n## Voting Tally\n\n# Code Review Voting Tally\n\n## Per-finding vote breakdown\n\n| Item | YES | NO | EXON | JERR | Result |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| FINDING_1 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | accepted |\n| FINDING_10 | 0 | 0 | 3 | 0 | exonerated |\n| FINDING_2 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | accepted |\n| FINDING_3 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | accepted |\n| FINDING_4 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 0 | rejected |\n| FINDING_5 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | accepted |\n| FINDING_6 | 0 | 0 | 3 | 0 | exonerated |\n| FINDING_7 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | accepted |\n| FINDING_8 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | accepted |\n| FINDING_9 | 0 | 0 | 3 | 0 | exonerated |\n\n## Reviewer Competition Scoreboard\n\n| Reviewer | Proposed | Accepted | Neutral/Exon | Rejected | OOS-Proposed | OOS-Accepted | OOS-Neutral/Exon | OOS-Rejected | Score | Status |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| cursor-specialist-correctness | 5 | 4 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 4 | STATUS=OK |\n| cursor-specialist-edge-cases | 5 | 5 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 5 | STATUS=OK |\n| cursor-specialist-plan-fidelity | 3 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 2 | STATUS=OK |\n| cursor-specialist-security | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | STATUS=OK |\n| cursor-specialist-structure | 5 | 4 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 3 | STATUS=OK |\n| cursor-specialist-testing | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | STATUS=OK |\n\n"}
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3136:./larch-logs/implement/EF527D59-EE0F-4DCC-B3E0-57277F4006D3/round-3/accepted-findings.md:15:- **Concern**: [important] Implementation plan specified replacing sort -u with cp so duplicate identical TSV rows still reach the LLM aggregator; branch uses awk '!seen[$0]++' which still drops identical full rows before aggregation. Two overlapping collector paths could emit the same title/label/body line twice; with awk they collapse to one pre-aggregator row, so the aggregator never sees the duplicate attribution the plan assumed would still exist. Use cp as planned if aggregator-owned dedup is desired, or keep awk and update plan/docs with explicit rationale for row-level dedup vs aggregator dedup.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3137:./larch-logs/implement/EF527D59-EE0F-4DCC-B3E0-57277F4006D3/round-3/findings.md:73:- **Concern**: [important] Implementation plan specified replacing sort -u with cp so duplicate identical TSV rows still reach the LLM aggregator; branch uses awk '!seen[$0]++' which still drops identical full rows before aggregation. Two overlapping collector paths could emit the same title/label/body line twice; with awk they collapse to one pre-aggregator row, so the aggregator never sees the duplicate attribution the plan assumed would still exist. Use cp as planned if aggregator-owned dedup is desired, or keep awk and update plan/docs with explicit rationale for row-level dedup vs aggregator dedup.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3138:./larch-logs/implement/9F04EDBB-9EBB-47C3-99BE-9C53289EE007/session-transcript.jsonl:40:{"turn":39,"role":"assistant","blocks":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_01PqiiPAd9yCLxfJeSJjt4X4","name":"Skill","input":{"skill":"larch:implement","args":"--merge --session-env <TMPDIR>/session-env.sh --issue 2485 Cutover: rework /implement and /design around the issue-anchored plan. Move the autonomy boundary from \"verbal idea → merged PR\" to \"vetted plan in issue → merged PR\". /design becomes the sole creative entry point and writes the plan to the issue body using the wire format `<!-- larch:plan:start -->\\n## Plan\\n...\\n## Acceptance\\n- ...\\n<!-- larch:plan:end -->`. /implement requires a pre-existing planned issue and runs a preflight adequacy check before touching anything. Clarification round-trip via `<!-- larch:clarify-request id=<N> -->` and `<!-- larch:clarify-response id=<N> -->` comments toggling the `needs-design-clarification` label. Collapse the SIMPLE/HARD review-flow split inside /implement into a single unified review flow with hard 5-round cap (down from base 7 + degraded retries), full 12-reviewer panel + 3-voter dialectic as only mode, dynamic-archetypes default cap 4→6 (max 8). /design rework: accept positional <issue-N> or verbal description (verbal creates tracking issue via /larch:issue first, forward --no-dedup if set); detect `needs-design-clarification` label → run lightweight clarify-loop (read latest unmatched larch:clarify-request, Q&A via AskUserQuestion + skill-less research, update larch:plan block, post larch:clarify-response, remove label, no panel/tier/approval gate); otherwise full design flow with tier from explicit flag: `--hard` (4 sketches + 10-reviewer panel + per-finding user approval), `--simple` (1-sketch main agent + 10-reviewer panel + auto-applied findings), `--trivial` (main-agent plan, no panel, no findings), no flag → AskUserQuestion with three tier options + brief explanations. Lightweight problem-actuality check (codebase grep) before sketches. Final user-approval gate (step f) for all tiers before writing plan via plan-block-write.sh. Add --no-dedup forwarded to /issue on verbal path. Remove flags from /design: --auto, --subagent, --inline, --quick, --full, --design-classification, --branch-info, --step-prefix. Remove the manifest-export path (heavy-worker subagent dispatch, MANIFEST_WRITTEN= line, post-design boundary continuation directive). /design on issue that already has a plan AND no clarify-request: explain situation, AskUserQuestion with three branches (re-run full design and replace block, ad-hoc edits via Q&A, cancel). /implement rework: require <issue-N> positional, reject verbal descriptions loudly pointing to /design; remove flags --auto, --quick, --inline, --design-only, --no-issues, --hard, --issue (positional now); preflight before session/branch/tmpdir does `gh issue view` with 2× retry then hard-fail, parses larch:plan block (refuse on malformed/missing), runs in-prompt plan-adequacy audit (main agent judges, must produce non-trivial concrete questions on refusal). On audit refusal: post larch:clarify-request via clarify-comment-post.sh, add needs-design-clarification label via clarify-label.sh, exit 0. On audit pass: session setup, adopt the issue (Branch 2 only, no Branch 4 create), write parsed plan to $IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/plan.txt, skip the old Step 1 design phase entirely. Step 5 onward unchanged in semantics but SIMPLE/HARD split removed. Remove anti-halt machinery tied to the dead design boundary (NEVER #12, NEVER #14, post-design-boundary wrapper coupling). Review-flow unification: single unified review flow uses full 12-reviewer panel + 3-voter dialectic (today's HARD path) as only mode; hard cap 5 review rounds; --dynamic-archetypes default cap 4→6 (max 8); run-step5-review.sh no longer takes panel-mode arg, review-and-fix.sh --panel hard becomes only mode or --panel flag dropped entirely. Step 5 entry breadcrumb collapses: drop \"— hard mode\" tag, replace \"up to 7 rounds [base 7 + degraded-round retries]\" with \"up to 5 rounds\", drop \"hard \" prefix on \"review panel\", update cap=4 to cap=6. Update all CI-pinned assertions referencing SIMPLE/HARD split (test-implement-structure.sh, test-fix-issue-bail-detection.sh, panel-mode literal pins, breadcrumb-text pins, dynamic-archetypes default-value pins). /fix-issue rework: drop --auto, --inline, --hard, deprecated --quick (--hard becomes meaningless after review-flow unification, --quick was already deprecated). In Step 5a, check plan presence via plan-block-read.sh — on miss post \"run /design first\" comment, release IN PROGRESS lock, skip to cleanup; on hit forward new positional <issue> arg to /implement. COMPLEXITY classification (Step 4) becomes informational-only since /implement no longer accepts tier flags; consider deleting SIMPLE/HARD label too if it carries no downstream signal. Wrappers: delete skills/imaq/ (the --merge --auto --quick alias) AND skills/imq/ (the --merge --quick alias) — both contain flags that no longer exist on /implement. Only /im (forwarding bare --merge) survives as an alias — verify it still forwards correctly to new /implement signature with positional issue number. Update .claude/skills/agnix-fix/SKILL.md: drop --auto and --quick, document plan-presence assumption. Acceptance: smoke-test on a real small issue end-to-end before declaring done (/design <N> --trivial → plan in issue body → /implement <N> → green merge); all CI-pinned literal assertions updated in lockstep; make lint and agent-lint pass. Dependency #2484 is now closed (helper scripts plan-block-read.sh, plan-block-write.sh, clarify-comment-post.sh, clarify-label.sh, clarify-state.sh exist)."}}]}
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3139:./larch-logs/implement/9F04EDBB-9EBB-47C3-99BE-9C53289EE007/session-transcript.jsonl:69:{"turn":68,"role":"assistant","blocks":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_01EiDiBLjEkjCfiHdMVK3EF3","name":"Skill","input":{"skill":"design","args":"--subagent --design-classification \"HARD\" --step-prefix \"1.::design plan::/implement\" --branch-info \"IS_MAIN=true IS_USER_BRANCH=false USER_PREFIX=sergey-zhupanov CURRENT_BRANCH=main\" --session-env <TMPDIR>/session-env.sh Cutover: rework /implement and /design around the issue-anchored plan (GitHub issue #2485). GOAL: move autonomy boundary from \"verbal idea → merged PR\" to \"vetted plan in issue → merged PR\". /design becomes sole creative entry point and writes plan to issue body. /implement requires pre-existing planned issue and runs preflight adequacy check before touching anything. Collapse SIMPLE/HARD review-flow split inside /implement into single unified review flow with hard 5-round cap. WIRE FORMAT: plan stored in issue body between `<!-- larch:plan:start -->` and `<!-- larch:plan:end -->` markers, containing `## Plan` (free-form markdown) and `## Acceptance` (bullet list) sections. Clarification round-trip via `<!-- larch:clarify-request id=<N> -->` comment from /implement toggling `needs-design-clarification` label on, matching `<!-- larch:clarify-response id=<N> -->` from /design toggling it off. /DESIGN REWORK: accept positional <issue-N> or verbal description; on verbal create tracking issue first via /larch:issue (forward --no-dedup if set); detect `needs-design-clarification` label → run lightweight clarify-loop (read latest unmatched larch:clarify-request, Q&A with user via AskUserQuestion + skill-less research via Read/Grep/Glob/WebFetch, update larch:plan block, post larch:clarify-response, remove label — no panel, no tier classification, no separate approval gate); otherwise full design flow with tier from explicit flag — `--hard` (4 sketches + 10-reviewer panel + per-finding user approval during apply-findings), `--simple` (1-sketch main agent + 10-reviewer panel + auto-applied findings — today's behavior), `--trivial` (main-agent plan, no panel, no findings), no flag → AskUserQuestion with all three tier options + brief explanations. Lightweight problem-actuality check (codebase grep for stated symbols) before sketches. Final user-approval gate (step f) for all tiers before writing plan to issue via plan-block-write.sh. Add --no-dedup forwarded to /issue on verbal-description path. Remove flags: --auto, --subagent, --inline, --quick, --full, --design-classification, --branch-info, --step-prefix. Remove manifest-export path (heavy-worker subagent dispatch, MANIFEST_WRITTEN= line, post-design boundary continuation directive). /design on issue that already has a plan AND no clarify-request: explain situation, AskUserQuestion with three branches — (a) re-run full design flow and replace block, (b) ad-hoc edits via Q&A, (c) cancel. /IMPLEMENT REWORK: require <issue-N> positional; reject verbal descriptions loudly pointing user at /design; remove flags --auto, --quick, --inline, --design-only, --no-issues, --hard, --issue (positional now); PREFLIGHT before session/branch/tmpdir does `gh issue view` with 2× retry on transient failure then hard-fail, parses larch:plan block (refuse on malformed or missing), runs in-prompt plan-adequacy audit (main agent judges, must produce non-trivial concrete questions on refusal — not \"is this what you want?\"); on audit refusal post larch:clarify-request via clarify-comment-post.sh, add needs-design-clarification label via clarify-label.sh, exit 0; on audit pass session setup, adopt the issue (Branch 2 only, no Branch 4 create), write parsed plan to $IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/plan.txt, skip the old Step 1 design phase entirely. Everything from Step 5 (review) onward unchanged in semantics but SIMPLE/HARD split removed. Remove anti-halt machinery tied to dead design boundary (NEVER #12, NEVER #14, post-design-boundary wrapper coupling). /IMPLEMENT REVIEW-FLOW UNIFICATION: single unified review flow uses full 12-reviewer panel + 3-voter dialectic (today's HARD path) as only mode — quality should not regress, simplification is in surface area not panel size; hard cap at 5 review rounds (down from today's HARD-path base of 7 + degraded-round retries) — no retry budget on top, 5 is absolute ceiling; increase default --dynamic-archetypes cap from 4 to 6 (max remains 8) — operators can still override via flag or LARCH_DYNAMIC_ARCHETYPES_MAX env var; run-step5-review.sh no longer takes panel-mode argument, review-and-fix.sh --panel hard becomes only mode (or --panel flag dropped entirely); Step 5 entry breadcrumb collapses to reflect unified flow — today's form \"🔶 /implement 5: code review — hard mode (review-and-fix.sh, up to 7 rounds [base 7 + degraded-round retries]; 3-judge panel on round 1 (Claude+Codex+Cursor), 2-judge on rounds 2+ (Claude+Cursor); hard review panel: 6 Cursor specialists; dynamic-archetypes cap=4)\" becomes \"🔶 /implement 5: code review (review-and-fix.sh, up to 5 rounds; 3-judge panel on round 1 (Claude+Codex+Cursor), 2-judge on rounds 2+ (Claude+Cursor); review panel: 6 Cursor specialists; dynamic-archetypes cap=6)\"; update all CI-pinned assertions referencing SIMPLE/HARD split (test-implement-structure.sh, test-fix-issue-bail-detection.sh, panel-mode literal pins, breadcrumb-text pins, dynamic-archetypes default-value pins). /FIX-ISSUE REWORK: drop --auto, --inline, --hard, deprecated --quick (--hard becomes meaningless after review-flow unification, --quick was already deprecated); in Step 5a check plan presence via plan-block-read.sh — on miss post \"run /design first\" comment, release IN PROGRESS lock, skip to cleanup; on hit forward new positional <issue> arg to /implement; COMPLEXITY classification (Step 4) becomes informational-only since /implement no longer accepts tier flags — consider deleting SIMPLE/HARD label too if no downstream signal. WRAPPERS: delete skills/imaq/ (the --merge --auto --quick alias) AND skills/imq/ (the --merge --quick alias) — both contain flags that no longer exist on /implement; only /im (forwarding bare --merge) survives as alias — verify it still forwards correctly to new /implement signature with positional issue number; update .claude/skills/agnix-fix/SKILL.md — drop --auto and --quick, document plan-presence assumption. ACCEPTANCE: smoke-test on a real small issue end-to-end before declaring done (`/design <N> --trivial` → plan in issue body → `/implement <N>` → green merge); all CI-pinned literal assertions updated in lockstep (test-fix-issue-bail-detection.sh, test-implement-structure.sh, anti-halt / anti-polling harnesses, any panel-mode literal pins); make lint and agent-lint pass. DEPENDENCY: previously blocked by helper-script issue #2484 (now CLOSED — plan-block-read.sh, plan-block-write.sh, clarify-comment-post.sh, clarify-label.sh, clarify-state.sh all exist in scripts/)."}}]}
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3140:./larch-logs/implement/9F04EDBB-9EBB-47C3-99BE-9C53289EE007/session-transcript.jsonl:90:{"turn":89,"role":"assistant","blocks":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_016dZ4BuMzHjyFCw2uYfSr66","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"<TMPDIR>/feature-description.txt","content":"Cutover: rework /implement and /design around the issue-anchored plan (GitHub issue #2485).\n\n# Goal\n\nMove the autonomy boundary from \"verbal idea → merged PR\" to \"vetted plan in issue → merged PR\". /design becomes the sole creative entry point and writes the plan to the issue body. /implement requires a pre-existing planned issue and runs a preflight adequacy check before touching anything. As part of the same cutover, the SIMPLE/HARD review-flow split inside /implement is collapsed into a single unified review flow with a hard 5-round cap.\n\n# Wire format\n\nPlan stored in issue body between `<!-- larch:plan:start -->` and `<!-- larch:plan:end -->` markers, containing `## Plan` (free-form markdown) and `## Acceptance` (bullet list) sections.\n\nClarification round-trip: `<!-- larch:clarify-request id=<N> -->` comment from /implement toggles the `needs-design-clarification` label on; matching `<!-- larch:clarify-response id=<N> -->` from /design toggles it off.\n\n# Operator-confirmed scope decisions (Step 1c)\n\n1. **Cutover scope**: FULL cutover in one PR — wire format + /implement + /design + /fix-issue + review unification + wrappers + CI updates.\n2. **--panel flag**: DROP entirely from review-and-fix.sh argv. Update every caller and harness that references --panel.\n3. **CI sweep**: EXHAUSTIVE. Plan a grep-driven pass over every `test-*.sh` and `agent-lint`-anchored prompt surface for SIMPLE/HARD/panel/cap=4 references; update each.\n4. **In-flight runs**: HARD CUTOVER at merge. No transition shim, no `--legacy-flags` accept-and-warn layer.\n\n# /design rework\n\n- Accept positional `<issue-N>` or verbal description. On verbal, create a tracking issue first via /larch:issue (forward `--no-dedup` if set on /design).\n- Detect `needs-design-clarification` label → run lightweight clarify-loop: read latest unmatched `larch:clarify-request`, Q&A with user (`AskUserQuestion` + skill-less research via Read/Grep/Glob/WebFetch), update the `larch:plan` block, post `larch:clarify-response`, remove the label. No panel, no tier classification, no separate approval gate.\n- Otherwise: full design flow. Tier comes from explicit flag:\n  - `--hard`: 4 sketches + 10-reviewer panel + per-finding user approval during apply-findings phase.\n  - `--simple`: 1-sketch (main agent) + 10-reviewer panel + auto-applied findings (today's behavior).\n  - `--trivial`: main-agent plan, no panel, no findings.\n  - No flag: AskUserQuestion with all three tier options + brief explanations of each.\n- Lightweight problem-actuality check (codebase grep for stated symbols) before sketches.\n- Final user-approval gate (step f) for all tiers before writing the plan to the issue via plan-block-write.sh.\n- Add `--no-dedup` flag forwarded to /issue on the verbal-description path.\n- Remove flags: --auto, --subagent, --inline, --quick, --full, --design-classification, --branch-info, --step-prefix. (The old --quick/--full shorthand for sketch fan-out is replaced by the explicit --trivial/--simple/--hard tier flags.)\n- Remove the manifest-export path (heavy-worker subagent dispatch, MANIFEST_WRITTEN= line, post-design boundary continuation directive).\n- /design on issue that already has a plan AND no clarify-request: explain the situation; AskUserQuestion with three branches — (a) re-run full design flow and replace block, (b) ad-hoc edits via Q&A, (c) cancel.\n\n# /implement rework\n\n- Require `<issue-N>` positional. Reject verbal descriptions loudly, pointing the user at /design.\n- Remove flags: --auto, --quick, --inline, --design-only, --no-issues, --hard, --issue (positional now).\n- **Preflight** (before session/branch/tmpdir): `gh issue view` with 2× retry on transient failure then hard-fail; parse `larch:plan` block (refuse on malformed or missing); run in-prompt plan-adequacy audit (main agent judges, must produce non-trivial concrete questions on refusal — not \"is this what you want?\").\n- On audit refusal: post `larch:clarify-request` via clarify-comment-post.sh, add `needs-design-clarification` label via clarify-label.sh, exit 0.\n- On audit pass: session setup, adopt the issue (Branch 2 only — no Branch 4 create), write parsed plan to `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/plan.txt`, skip the old Step 1 design phase entirely.\n- Everything from Step 5 (review) onward unchanged in semantics, but the SIMPLE/HARD split is removed — see next section.\n- Remove anti-halt machinery tied to the dead design boundary (NEVER #12, NEVER #14, post-design-boundary wrapper coupling).\n\n# /implement review-flow unification\n\nThe current Step 5 has two divergent paths gated on `--quick`: SIMPLE (a `--panel simple` argv) and HARD (a `--panel hard` argv with the full 12-reviewer panel + 3-voter dialectic, base 7 rounds + degraded-round retries). With `--quick` gone, the split has no driver. Collapse them:\n\n- Single unified review flow uses the full 12-reviewer panel + 3-voter dialectic (today's HARD path) as the only mode. Quality should not regress; the simplification is in surface area, not panel size.\n- **Hard cap at 5 review rounds** (down from today's HARD-path base of 7 + degraded-round retries). No retry budget on top — 5 is the absolute ceiling.\n- **Increase the default `--dynamic-archetypes` cap from 4 to 6** (max remains 8). Operators can still override via the flag or the `LARCH_DYNAMIC_ARCHETYPES_MAX` env var.\n- `run-step5-review.sh` no longer takes a panel-mode argument; `--panel` is DROPPED entirely from review-and-fix.sh (per operator decision).\n- Step 5 entry breadcrumb collapses to reflect the unified flow. Today's form:\n\n  ```\n  🔶 /implement 5: code review — hard mode (review-and-fix.sh, up to 7 rounds [base 7 + degraded-round retries]; 3-judge panel on round 1 (Claude+Codex+Cursor), 2-judge on rounds 2+ (Claude+Cursor); hard review panel: 6 Cursor specialists; dynamic-archetypes cap=4)\n  ```\n\n  becomes:\n\n  ```\n  🔶 /implement 5: code review (review-and-fix.sh, up to 5 rounds; 3-judge panel on round 1 (Claude+Codex+Cursor), 2-judge on rounds 2+ (Claude+Cursor); review panel: 6 Cursor specialists; dynamic-archetypes cap=6)\n  ```\n\n  Changes from today: drop the ` — hard mode` tag, replace `up to 7 rounds [base 7 + degraded-round retries]` with `up to 5 rounds`, drop the `hard ` prefix on `review panel`, update `cap=4` to `cap=6`.\n\n- Update all CI-pinned assertions that referenced the SIMPLE/HARD split (test-implement-structure.sh, test-fix-issue-bail-detection.sh, any panel-mode literal pins, any breadcrumb-text pins, any `dynamic-archetypes` default-value pins) — operator chose EXHAUSTIVE sweep.\n\n# /fix-issue rework\n\n- Drop --auto, --inline, --hard, deprecated --quick. (--hard becomes meaningless after the review-flow unification above; --quick was already deprecated.)\n- In Step 5a, check plan presence via plan-block-read.sh. On miss: post a \"run /design first\" comment, release the IN PROGRESS lock, skip to cleanup. On hit: forward new positional `<issue>` arg to /implement.\n- COMPLEXITY classification (Step 4) becomes informational-only — no flag is forwarded to /implement since /implement no longer accepts tier flags. Consider deleting the SIMPLE/HARD label too if it carries no downstream signal.\n\n# Wrappers\n\n- Delete skills/imaq/ (the `--merge --auto --quick` alias) AND skills/imq/ (the `--merge --quick` alias) — both contain flags that no longer exist on /implement.\n- Only /im (forwarding bare `--merge`) survives as an alias. Verify it still forwards correctly to the new /implement signature with positional issue number.\n- Update .claude/skills/agnix-fix/SKILL.md: drop --auto and --quick; document plan-presence assumption.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- Smoke-test on a real small issue end-to-end before declaring done: `/design <N> --trivial` → plan in issue body → `/implement <N>` → green merge. (This is post-merge operator validation, NOT part of this PR's CI.)\n- All CI-pinned literal assertions updated in lockstep (test-fix-issue-bail-detection.sh, test-implement-structure.sh, anti-halt / anti-polling harnesses, any panel-mode literal pins) — EXHAUSTIVE sweep.\n- `make lint` and `agent-lint` pass.\n\n# Dependency\n\nPreviously blocked by helper-script issue #2484 — NOW CLOSED. Helper scripts present in scripts/: plan-block-read.sh, plan-block-write.sh, clarify-comment-post.sh, clarify-label.sh, clarify-state.sh, plus test-plan-block.sh, test-clarify-comment.sh, test-clarify-state.sh.\n"}}]}
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3141:./larch-logs/implement/9F04EDBB-9EBB-47C3-99BE-9C53289EE007/session-transcript.jsonl:99:{"turn":98,"role":"assistant","blocks":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_013V6QZC9UzdS2G4vyYWY9Ef","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"<TMPDIR>/feature-description.txt","content":"Cutover: rework /implement and /design around the issue-anchored plan (GitHub issue #2485).\n\n# Goal\n\nMove the autonomy boundary from \"verbal idea → merged PR\" to \"vetted plan in issue → merged PR\". /design becomes the sole creative entry point and writes the plan to the issue body. /implement requires a pre-existing planned issue and runs a preflight adequacy check before touching anything.\n\n# Operator-confirmed scope refinement (Step 1c + mid-design clarification)\n\nThe original issue body described the review-flow unification as a thing to do. The operator clarified that work has already landed:\n\n- ✅ ALREADY DONE: `run-step5-review.sh` maps both `POST_PLAN_WORKFLOW_PATH=SIMPLE` and `=HARD` to `REVIEW_PANEL=\"hard\"` (line 147, 152). Comment at line 148 says \"Base Step 5 round cap is intentionally 5 for SIMPLE and HARD (unified panel)\".\n- ✅ ALREADY DONE: round_cap = 5 for both (run-step5-review.sh + SKILL.md confirm).\n- ✅ ALREADY DONE: `--dynamic-archetypes` default = 6 (review-and-fix.sh line 924; SKILL.md line 156).\n- ✅ ALREADY DONE: Step 5 entry breadcrumb already updated to the \"unified hard panel ... up to $effective_round_cap rounds [base 5 + degraded-round retries] ... cap=$dynamic_archetypes_cap\" form (SKILL.md line 1363).\n- ✅ ALREADY DONE: `skills/imaq/` and `skills/imq/` already removed from the tree.\n\nWhat is LEFT in the review-flow space:\n- ❌ DROP `--panel` flag entirely from `skills/review-and-fix/scripts/review-and-fix.sh` argv (operator decision). Currently the flag still exists at lines 37 (usage), 70 (parser), 553, 901, 993 — all argv plumbing that is dead now that there's only one panel mode. Also drop the `--panel \"$REVIEW_PANEL\"` pass-through at `scripts/run-step5-review.sh:179` and the local `REVIEW_PANEL` variable.\n- ❌ EXHAUSTIVE CI assertion sweep: grep all `test-*.sh` and `agent-lint`-anchored prompt surfaces for `--panel`, `panel hard`, `panel simple`, `simple|hard`, `cap=4`, `up to 7 rounds`, `hard mode`, `hard review panel`, SIMPLE/HARD split prose, etc., and update each.\n\nOperator-confirmed scope decisions (Step 1c):\n\n1. **Cutover scope**: FULL cutover in one PR.\n2. **--panel flag**: DROP entirely.\n3. **CI sweep**: EXHAUSTIVE.\n4. **In-flight runs**: HARD CUTOVER. No transition shim.\n\n# Big remaining work (the architectural cutover)\n\n## Wire format (new, agreed)\n\nPlan stored in issue body between `<!-- larch:plan:start -->` and `<!-- larch:plan:end -->` markers, containing `## Plan` (free-form markdown) and `## Acceptance` (bullet list) sections.\n\nClarification round-trip: `<!-- larch:clarify-request id=<N> -->` comment from /implement toggles the `needs-design-clarification` label on; matching `<!-- larch:clarify-response id=<N> -->` from /design toggles it off.\n\nHelper scripts already exist (#2484 closed): `scripts/plan-block-read.sh`, `scripts/plan-block-write.sh`, `scripts/clarify-comment-post.sh`, `scripts/clarify-label.sh`, `scripts/clarify-state.sh`, plus offline harnesses `test-plan-block.sh`, `test-clarify-comment.sh`, `test-clarify-state.sh`.\n\n## /design rework\n\n- Accept positional `<issue-N>` or verbal description. On verbal, create a tracking issue first via /larch:issue (forward `--no-dedup` if set on /design).\n- Detect `needs-design-clarification` label → run lightweight clarify-loop: read latest unmatched `larch:clarify-request`, Q&A with user (`AskUserQuestion` + skill-less research via Read/Grep/Glob/WebFetch), update the `larch:plan` block, post `larch:clarify-response`, remove the label. No panel, no tier classification, no separate approval gate.\n- Otherwise: full design flow. Tier comes from explicit flag:\n  - `--hard`: 4 sketches + 10-reviewer panel + per-finding user approval during apply-findings phase.\n  - `--simple`: 1-sketch (main agent) + 10-reviewer panel + auto-applied findings (today's behavior).\n  - `--trivial`: main-agent plan, no panel, no findings.\n  - No flag: AskUserQuestion with all three tier options + brief explanations of each.\n- Lightweight problem-actuality check (codebase grep for stated symbols) before sketches.\n- Final user-approval gate (step f) for all tiers before writing the plan to the issue via plan-block-write.sh.\n- Add `--no-dedup` flag forwarded to /issue on the verbal-description path.\n- Remove flags: --auto, --subagent, --inline, --quick, --full, --design-classification, --branch-info, --step-prefix. The old --quick/--full shorthand for sketch fan-out is replaced by the explicit --trivial/--simple/--hard tier flags.\n- Remove the manifest-export path (heavy-worker subagent dispatch, MANIFEST_WRITTEN= line, post-design boundary continuation directive).\n- /design on issue that already has a plan AND no clarify-request: explain the situation; AskUserQuestion with three branches — (a) re-run full design flow and replace block, (b) ad-hoc edits via Q&A, (c) cancel.\n\n## /implement rework\n\n- Require `<issue-N>` positional. Reject verbal descriptions loudly, pointing the user at /design.\n- Remove flags: --auto, --quick, --inline, --design-only, --no-issues, --hard, --issue (positional now).\n- **Preflight** (before session/branch/tmpdir): `gh issue view` with 2× retry on transient failure then hard-fail; parse `larch:plan` block via `plan-block-read.sh` (refuse on malformed or missing); run in-prompt plan-adequacy audit (main agent judges, must produce non-trivial concrete questions on refusal — not \"is this what you want?\").\n- On audit refusal: post `larch:clarify-request` via clarify-comment-post.sh, add `needs-design-clarification` label via clarify-label.sh, exit 0.\n- On audit pass: session setup, adopt the issue (Branch 2 only — no Branch 4 create), write parsed plan to `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/plan.txt`, skip the old Step 1 design phase entirely.\n- Everything from Step 5 (review) onward unchanged in semantics.\n- Remove anti-halt machinery tied to the dead design boundary: NEVER #12, NEVER #14, the post-design-boundary wrapper (`skills/implement/scripts/post-design-boundary.sh`), Stop hook coupling, and any harnesses pinning those literals.\n- Drop the `--panel \"$REVIEW_PANEL\"` pass-through at `scripts/run-step5-review.sh:179` and the local `REVIEW_PANEL` variable (since `--panel` is being removed from review-and-fix.sh argv).\n\n## /fix-issue rework\n\n- Drop --auto, --inline, --hard, deprecated --quick. (--hard becomes meaningless after the review-flow unification; --quick was already deprecated.)\n- In Step 5a, check plan presence via plan-block-read.sh. On miss: post a \"run /design first\" comment, release the IN PROGRESS lock, skip to cleanup. On hit: forward new positional `<issue>` arg to /implement.\n- COMPLEXITY classification (Step 4) becomes informational-only — no flag is forwarded to /implement since /implement no longer accepts tier flags. Consider deleting the SIMPLE/HARD label too if it carries no downstream signal.\n\n## Wrappers\n\n- Confirm `skills/imaq/` and `skills/imq/` are already removed (verified above). Verify the marketplace `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` / topology don't reference them.\n- Only /im (forwarding bare `--merge`) survives. Verify it still forwards correctly to the new /implement signature with positional issue number.\n- Update `.claude/skills/agnix-fix/SKILL.md`: drop --auto and --quick; document plan-presence assumption (agnix-fix consumes an already-planned issue via /implement positional).\n\n## Drop --panel from review-and-fix.sh\n\n- `skills/review-and-fix/scripts/review-and-fix.sh`:\n  - Line 37: usage string remove `--panel simple|hard`.\n  - Line 70: argv parser case for `--panel`.\n  - Line 553: `[[ \"$panel\" == \"simple\" || \"$panel\" == \"hard\" ]]` guard (the function this protects, plus any callers, should treat panel as always hard).\n  - Line 901: `--panel must be simple or hard` validation.\n  - Line 993: `--panel \"$PANEL\"` in nested invocations of itself (re-review loop).\n  - Any internal `PANEL` variable references — most should be deletable; some may need `panel=\"hard\"` literal substitution.\n- `scripts/run-step5-review.sh`:\n  - Line 147, 152: REVIEW_PANEL=\"hard\" assignment (delete both).\n  - Line 179: `--panel \"$REVIEW_PANEL\"` argv pass-through (delete).\n  - The REVIEW_PANEL local variable (delete).\n\n## CI assertion sweep (exhaustive)\n\nGrep targets across `**/test-*.sh`, `**/agent-lint*.toml`, and `**/SKILL.md`:\n- `--panel`, `panel hard`, `panel simple`, `panel_mode`, `PANEL=`, `REVIEW_PANEL=`\n- `simple|hard`, `simple\\|hard`\n- `cap=4` (now should be cap=6 if not already)\n- `up to 7 rounds`, `base 7 + degraded-round retries`, `hard mode` (now should be removed; only \"unified hard panel\" appears)\n- `hard review panel` (drop the `hard ` prefix per issue)\n- Anti-halt SIMPLE/HARD literal pins related to NEVER #12 / NEVER #14 (test-anti-improvised-wakeup.sh and others)\n- `--hard`, `--auto`, `--quick`, `--inline`, `--design-only`, `--no-issues` (now removed from /implement)\n- `--design-classification`, `--branch-info`, `--step-prefix`, `--subagent`, `--full` (now removed from /design)\n- `--issue` flag (now positional in /implement)\n- `MANIFEST_WRITTEN`, `post-design-boundary` (removed)\n- `skills/imaq`, `skills/imq` (removed)\n\nEach occurrence must be updated, deleted, or replaced with the new contract.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- Smoke-test on a real small issue end-to-end before declaring done: `/design <N> --trivial` → plan in issue body → `/implement <N>` → green merge. (Post-merge operator validation, NOT part of this PR's CI.)\n- All CI-pinned literal assertions updated in lockstep — EXHAUSTIVE sweep.\n- `make lint` and `agent-lint` pass.\n- `make test-design-manifest` (if it still exists), `make test-implement-structure`, `make test-fix-issue-bail-detection`, and any related harnesses all pass.\n- No grep hits for the legacy flags / SIMPLE-HARD split / panel-mode literals in the runtime surfaces or CI surfaces (operator-defined exhaustive sweep).\n\n# Dependency\n\nPreviously blocked by helper-script issue #2484 — NOW CLOSED. Helper scripts present in scripts/: plan-block-read.sh, plan-block-write.sh, clarify-comment-post.sh, clarify-label.sh, clarify-state.sh, plus test-plan-block.sh, test-clarify-comment.sh, test-clarify-state.sh.\n"}}]}
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3142:./larch-logs/implement/9F04EDBB-9EBB-47C3-99BE-9C53289EE007/plan-goals-test.md:47:5. **Already-planned branch**: if the issue body already contains a `larch:plan` block AND no `larch:clarify-request` is unmatched, run an `AskUserQuestion` with three branches: (a) re-run full design flow and replace block, (b) ad-hoc edits via Q&A, (c) cancel. Each branch's downstream behavior is documented inline.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3143:./larch-logs/implement/9F04EDBB-9EBB-47C3-99BE-9C53289EE007/plan-goals-test.md:166:2. Document the **plan-presence assumption**: "agnix-fix consumes an already-planned issue via `/implement <issue-N>` positional. If the upstream issue has no plan, the operator must run `/design <issue-N>` first."
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3144:./larch-logs/implement/EF527D59-EE0F-4DCC-B3E0-57277F4006D3/round-3/review-round-summary.md:25:- **Concern**: [important] Implementation plan specified replacing sort -u with cp so duplicate identical TSV rows still reach the LLM aggregator; branch uses awk '!seen[$0]++' which still drops identical full rows before aggregation. Two overlapping collector paths could emit the same title/label/body line twice; with awk they collapse to one pre-aggregator row, so the aggregator never sees the duplicate attribution the plan assumed would still exist. Use cp as planned if aggregator-owned dedup is desired, or keep awk and update plan/docs with explicit rationale for row-level dedup vs aggregator dedup.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3145:./larch-logs/implement/AD2E81EA-F212-47D8-B5C0-9F7AC6413215/plan-goals-test.md:7:Lower the "coder simplicity override" threshold in `skills/implement/SKILL.md` from `diff_lines < 30` (bypass external coder for changes under 30 lines) to `diff_lines <= 3` (bypass external coder only for truly trivial ≤3-line changes). This ensures the main agent only self-implements when the planned change is ≤3 lines; all larger changes are delegated to an external coder.
<TMPDIR>/codex-primary-plan-requirements-output.txt.sidecar:3146:./larch-logs/implement/5F7568AE-E8DA-4B76-8E08-E03C1DA604FC/round-2/findings.md:51:### FINDING_11: **`risk-integration` (plan / requirements)** — **Important** — [`skills/implement/SKILL.md`](skills/implement/SKILL.md) (Step 7a prose, ~lines where the pre-bump flush is documented): The umbrella `<feature_description>` and plan Part B require calling `write-final-report.sh` **before** merge (between Step 7a and Step 8) so `final-summary.md` is included in the pre-bump `larch-log` commit and is not suppressed by the post-merge sentinel. The branch instead **explicitly forbids** calling `write-final-report.sh` in Step 7a and points operators to Step 17/18 ([`skills/implement/scripts/write-final-report.md`](skills/implement/scripts/write-final-report.md) updated the same way). **Concrete scenario:** A successful merge still follows the old timing: `write-final-report` after `ship-pr.sh` creates `post-merge-sentinel`, so `larch-log.sh commit` can still refuse to pick up `final-summary.md` for the PR-embedded run-log—exactly the regression Part B was meant to fix. **Suggested fix:** Either implement the planned pre-bump call (accepting `PR: N/A` in that snapshot) or amend the issue/feature text and add an alternate fix (e.g. teach `larch-log.sh` / `refresh-run-logs.sh` to commit `final-summary` before the sentinel) and add a regression test that reproduces the sentinel interaction.
